  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "HCLTech keeps growing modestly ahead of TCS/Infosys in constant currency (4-5% USD) as AI-led deal wins and bolt-on M&A roughly offset AI-driven deflation in legacy application and BPO work; EBIT stays within the 17.5-18.5% guided band and near-full payout continues. INR revenue +9% in FY27 (rupee tailwind, H1 delivered) fading to 5.5% by year 10.",
    "bull": "HCLTech's early AI positioning (Advanced AI revenue $876M annualized, OpenAI/NVIDIA partnerships, AI Force across 75+ accounts) and engineering heritage capture the semiconductor/Physical-AI outsourcing wave: record bookings translate into 6-7% USD growth for five years, HCLSoftware stabilises, and outcome-based pricing lifts adj. EBITDA to 23%.",
    "bear": "The company's own flagged risk materialises: AI-native delivery and client insourcing deflate labour-arbitrage revenue faster than new AI streams scale, HCLSoftware keeps declining, US/Europe discretionary IT budgets stay weak and visa costs rise. USD revenue stalls for three years and margins compress ~2.5pp as pricing power erodes while AI-talent costs rise."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Owner-FCF DCF in INR: unlevered FCF after SBC and full lease cash, discounted at a 12% INR cost of capital, plus ~29,300 crore of treasury investments. HCLTech is a mature, cash-generative, near-SBC-free services compounder returning ~90%+ of earnings; value hinges on whether 4-5% USD growth and ~18% EBIT survive AI deflation of labour-based services.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "AI/GenAI deflation of labour-based ADM, BPO and testing revenue (company-flagged, high severity)",
      "INR/USD path: INR-denominated growth assumes ~2-3% annual rupee depreciation; a stable rupee lowers INR revenue growth roughly one-for-one",
      "HCLSoftware decline (-4.1% cc FY26) eroding the highest-margin segment",
      "US/Europe discretionary IT-spend weakness and H-1B fee/immigration policy",
      "Terminal value ~2/3 of EV; 12% INR WACC vs 5% terminal growth drives most of the value"
